"The prognosis following radical prostatectomy is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+3 with tertiary pattern 4 (ISUP grade group 1), negative surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. The absence of lymph nodes removed introduces uncertainty, as nodal sampling was not performed, meaning the pNx status does not equate to pN0. Biopsy results showed a discrepancy, with the first session showing a cribriform pattern and the second showing perineural invasion, which complicates the assessment of local disease extent.\n\nCountervailing factors include the negative margins and the absence of lymphovascular invasion. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 11.7. The primary uncertainty lies in the postoperative PSA trajectory and subsequent follow-up, as this information is currently missing. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trend would significantly revise the prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence, but this is not a calibrated prediction. Missing nodal sampling widens the uncertainty regarding regional disease control. The fixed estimated horizon is 57.70 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.51–77.90 months; its coverage has not been validated."
